IDS with EoE
============

Shortcut
--------

IDS are used to detect intruders. They can be hostbased,
like EoE, or network-based (something similar to sniffers).
There are freely available net-based IDS like 'Snort'.
Hostbased IDS usually look what activities are done by local users,
such as logfile-analyzation etc. 
With EoE it is posible for you to do this in 'real-time'.


EoE now is a set of tools and scripts which
you can use to _build _your_ own IDS_.


Information that EoE provides
-----------------------------

The basic output-string from EoE looks like:

    <UID>:<EUID>:<PID>:<P_PROG>:<PROGRAM> 	where
    
	* UID is the programs UID
	* EUID it's EUID
	* PID  it's PID
	* P_PROG it's parent's short-programname, 'inetd' for example
	* PROGRAM the full pathname and arguments of the executed programs
	
It is up to you to use these information properly to build an IDS.
It is clear for what to use EUID and UID.

Weak Pathes (WP)
----------------

Now something new. A WP is a way (a path)
how programs are executed that they shouldn't.
    For example a /bin/sh started with EUID 0 from the pop3 daemon
is something strange, isn't it? So i would call the 'path' this
shell was executed 'weak'. Such WP now can be detected with EoE.

(It's maybe not a 100% secure solution, but the EoE IDS is a
try to do bring some scientific and analyzation into 
security-concepts and programs.)

So a WP-string maybe looks like

    1000:0:666:portmap:/bin/sh
    
that is a /bin/sh executed with EUID 0, from 'within' the
portmap-daemon. Would you call this a normal way? No. :-)

Rather it is an (sucessfull) attempt to break into your system, that
you have detected. Specifieng a rule that says

    "Kill all shells that are originated from portmap."

would terminate the intruders session. But how's this rule written down
in code? Remeber the PID ...


Using regular expressions (re)
------------------------------

I assume you know what re are.
Handling strings with regular expressions is simple in perl,
so i chose to use a perl-script to specify some rules for
the IDS. For example:

...
%bad_progz = (

    # warn on a rootshell
	WARN => ["[0-9]+:0:[0-9]+:.+:.+sh"],

    # kill rootshells executed by inetd directly,
    # this is rather an overflow
	KILL => ["[0-9]+:0:[0-9]+:inetd:.+sh"]
);
...

This is part of eoe.pl, and says nothing but:

	"WARN me whenever someone executes a rootshell on my box and"
	"KILL all rootshells that are executed from inetd."


Actually eoe.pl provides 3 levels:

    WARN, KILL and NOTICE (see eoe.pl).

It is up to you to give the script the rigth set of rules
to protect your system, eoe.pl is just a template for you.
I think you are very flexible then, you can even write
your total own scripts. 
Please note that i'm not a perl-hacker, so some things
maybe looks i use perl like C. :-)


Conclusion
----------

So, i think it is enuff said to make you able to write
down your own protection-scripts if you only know
a little bit about perl.
Please send feedback to me (krahmer@cs.uni-potsdam.de),
own scripts and interesting thoughts about EoE.






 





